summary: What happens when regulators and venues move the price grid. Wider ticks thicken queues and displayed depth but widen spreads; narrower ticks tighten spreads but thin out displayed size and make quotes flicker, so neither direction is free. The evidence from real experiments (the US Tick Size Pilot of 2016–18, Japan's TOPIX 100 tick cuts in 2014, and MiFID II's tick tables from 2018, later extended to systematic internalisers); why round numbers attract take-profit orders and stop-losses just beyond them (Osler), and how that makes prices stall and then jump; the ways traders get around the grid (midpoint trades, rebates, sub-penny price improvement); and tick changes in FX (EBS's switch from tenths of a pip back to half-pips in 2012) and Treasuries (the 2-year note's tick halved in 2018).
lede: 'The Price Grid showed that the tick decides which game market makers play. So who decides the tick, and what happens when they change it? This note looks at the real experiments where regulators and venues moved the grid, finds out who won and who lost, and then looks at two things the grid creates by accident: piles of orders at round numbers, and a whole industry of ways to trade between the ticks.'
