{% sec 1, "Moving the Grid", "What happens to a market when its tick gets bigger or smaller?", "Moving the grid" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Think about bus stops. If stops are a mile apart, crowds build up at each one and you might walk a long way to reach one, but each bus fills up quickly and runs fast. If there's a stop every ten metres, you never walk far, but buses stop constantly, nobody waits in a proper queue and the ride is jerky. Neither spacing is "right": it depends on how many people use the route.</p>
    <p>The tick works the same way. Deltashark's summary of the evidence is that changing it in either direction has costs:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="numbers">
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Wider tick</th><th>Narrower tick</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Quoted spread</td><td>Wider, if the tick was already near the spread</td><td>Narrower, if the old tick was holding it up</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Size shown at the best price</td><td>More: orders crowd onto fewer price levels</td><td>Less: the same orders spread over more levels</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Queues</td><td>Longer; time priority matters more</td><td>Shorter; price competition matters more</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Quotes</td><td>Change less often</td><td>Change much more often</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Who tends to gain</td><td>Market makers at the front of queues</td><td>Small orders crossing the spread</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Who tends to lose</td><td>Small orders paying the wider spread</td><td>Anyone who wanted to see real size at one price; slow traders who get pennied</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Because neither direction is free, Deltashark says tick regimes get <strong>recalibrated from time to time rather than settled once</strong>. You've already seen one recalibration: the US decision in 2024 to allow half-penny ticks for stocks whose spreads are usually tight, covered in Designated Makers, Fees and Special Markets. The interactive below shows the trade-off for three kinds of stock.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="lab">
    <div class="lab-head"><h3>Widen or shrink the tick</h3><p>Pick a stock by how wide market makers would quote it with no grid at all, then move the tick. The tone line is what a small order pays to cross (half the spread). The grey line is what makers actually need, so the gap between them is spare profit for whoever is at the front of the queue. The numbers are illustrative.</p></div>
